2026-10-05 11:12:37,677 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "en", "question": "How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?", "retrieval_queries": ["How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (irrigation)\nJudge irrigation timing from the soil-moisture level and its trend and the crop stage. Say whether to irrigate now, wait, or hold, and why.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Ravi\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-10-05 05:42 UTC (node field-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: n/a, n/a\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-10-05 05:42 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.4 C\n- Humidity: 70.0 %\n- Soil moisture: 45.0 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nNo active alerts.\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- General guidance: irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended, with frequency varying by crop phase — approximately once every 7 days during germination (1–35 days after planting, DAP), once every 10 days during tillering (36–100 DAP), once every 7 days during grand growth (101–270 DAP), and once every 15 days during maturity (271 DAP to harvest), adjusted for rainfall.\n- Subtropical regions typically need 6–8 irrigations across the season; tropical regions (including Karnataka) may need 20–36 irrigations due to climatic conditions, annual rainfall, planting season, crop duration, and soil type.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Summary:\nSugarcane is a long-duration, water-intensive crop requiring 1,500–2,500 mm of water over its growing cycle depending on climate. IISR documents phase-wise irrigation schedules, state-wise irrigation requirements, and three water-economizing techniques (skip-furrow, sprinkler, drip) that can cut water use by 30–95% while maintaining or improving productivity — highly relevant for Karnataka, which has one of the highest irrigation-water requirements among sugarcane-growing states in India.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Key Facts:\n- Total seasonal water requirement: 1,500–2,500 mm, depending on climate.\n- Karnataka irrigation requirement: 32 irrigations/season; 266 kg water per kg sugar produced.\n- Skip-furrow irrigation: 30–40% water saving.\n- Sprinkler irrigation: 50–60% water economy; drip irrigation: up to 95% application uniformity.\n- Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- **Karnataka-specific data**: 32 irrigations required per season; average yield 85.5 t/ha; irrigation water required to produce 1.0 kg of sugar is 266 kg — among the highest of all sugarcane states reported, reflecting Karnataka's tropical multi-irrigation cropping pattern.\n- **Net irrigation water requirement by technique (Karnataka, mm/year)**: furrow irrigation 1,878 mm; skip furrow 1,408 mm; sprinkler 1,325 mm; drip 1,186 mm — showing drip irrigation can reduce net water requirement by roughly 37% compared to conventional furrow irrigation for the same crop water demand.\n\n### Water-economizing methods\n1. **Skip-furrow/alternate furrow irrigation**: one row is skipped and irrigation applied only to alternate furrows, saving 30–40% water without reducing cane productivity.\n\n# USER QUESTION\nHow deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?", "farmer": "How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.9124}, "retrieved": [{"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information", "score": 0.9124, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Summary", "score": 0.9017, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Key Facts", "score": 0.8958, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information", "score": 0.891, "via": "original"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation:**  \nThe reference text states that the recommended irrigation depth per application for sugarcane is **7–8 cm**.\n\n**Analysis:**  \nApplying water to a depth of 7–8 cm per irrigation aligns with the agronomic guidelines for meeting the crop’s water demand while avoiding excess or deficit.\n\n**Confidence:**  \nHigh – the depth is explicitly given in the agricultural knowledge source.\n\n**Recommendations:**  \n\n- **Immediate actions:**  \n  - Apply irrigation water so that the infiltrated depth is between **7 cm and 8 cm** for each irrigation event.\n\n- **Monitoring advice:**  \n  - After irrigation, check soil‑moisture sensors or manually probe the soil to confirm that the moisture profile corresponds to the applied depth.  \n  - Observe the crop for any signs of water stress (wilting) or waterlogging (yellowing, root rot) and adjust future applications accordingly.\n\n- **When to seek expert help:**  \n  - If soil‑moisture readings remain consistently above or below the expected range after applying the 7–8 cm depth.  \n  - If you encounter unusual weather patterns, water‑availability constraints, or suspect irrigation system inefficiencies that prevent achieving the target depth.", "claims": ["supported: Recommended irrigation depth per application is 7–8 cm", "supported: A